 ],
 "captions": [
  {
   "start": 0.0,
   "end": 3.019,
   "text": "In Kentucky, a piece of paper fixed to a door\nwas service."
  },
  {
   "start": 3.069,
   "end": 6.57,
   "text": "The question is whether the paper stayed up long\nenough to be read."
  },
  {
   "start": 8.12,
   "end": 12.664,
   "text": "The case they brought is called Greene v. Lindsey.\nGreene is the sheriff."
  },
  {
   "start": 12.714,
   "end": 16.561,
   "text": "Lindsey is a tenant. The case is about a piece\nof paper."
  },
  {
   "start": 18.111,
   "end": 22.889,
   "text": "In 1975, the Housing Authority of Louisville began\nproceedings against three"
  },
  {
   "start": 22.639,
   "end": 23.702,
   "text": "of its tenants."
  },
  {
   "start": 23.752,
   "end": 29.444,
   "text": "The Court's opinion names them once: Linnie\nLindsey, Barbara Hodgens and Pamela Ray,"
  },
  {
   "start": 29.194,
   "end": 32.036,
   "text": "tenants in a Louisville housing project."
  },
  {
   "start": 32.086,
   "end": 37.605,
   "text": "That is very nearly everything the opinion says\nabout them as people. No ages."
  },
  {
   "start": 37.655,
   "end": 40.342,
   "text": "No jobs. No families."
  },
  {
   "start": 40.392,
   "end": 45.518,
   "text": "No rent figures, no arrears, no account of what\nthey were supposed to have done."
  },
  {
   "start": 45.568,
   "end": 47.861,
   "text": "Three names and a housing project."
  },
  {
   "start": 47.911,
   "end": 51.158,
   "text": "The question in front of the courts was never\nwhether the three"
  },
  {
   "start": 50.908,
   "end": 52.341,
   "text": "of them were good tenants."
  },
  {
   "start": 52.391,
   "end": 57.749,
   "text": "It was whether a particular piece of paper, fixed\nto a particular door, counted."
  },
  {
   "start": 57.799,
   "end": 62.089,
   "text": "Under the procedure, an officer went\nto an apartment door in Louisville and knocked."
  },
  {
   "start": 62.139,
   "end": 63.473,
   "text": "If nobody answered,"
  },
  {
   "start": 63.223,
   "end": 66.661,
   "text": "he took a piece of paper and fixed it\nto the outside of the door."
  },
  {
   "start": 66.711,
   "end": 71.559,
   "text": "Under Kentucky law, once the paper\nwas on the door, the tenant had been served."
  },
  {
   "start": 71.609,
   "end": 73.113,
   "text": "The clock started."
  },
  {
   "start": 73.163,
   "end": 77.593,
   "text": "The hearing would happen whether or not anyone\ninside ever read a word of it."
  },
  {
   "start": 77.643,
   "end": 81.051,
   "text": "Three tenants said that is exactly what happened\nto them."
  },
  {
   "start": 81.101,
   "end": 83.394,
   "text": "That they never saw the paper."
  },
  {
   "start": 83.444,
   "end": 87.874,
   "text": "That the first they knew of any of it\nwas when the writs of possession were served."
  },
  {
   "start": 87.924,
   "end": 89.614,
   "text": "That is their account."
  },
  {
   "start": 89.664,
   "end": 93.292,
   "text": "The proceeding was a forcible entry and detainer\naction —"
  },
  {
   "start": 93.042,
   "end": 97.205,
   "text": "the summary process a landlord uses to get\npossession back quickly."
  },
  {
   "start": 97.255,
   "end": 98.898,
   "text": "Speed is the point of it."
  },
  {
   "start": 98.948,
   "end": 102.031,
   "text": "Service of the court's notice fell\nto the sheriff's office."
  },
  {
   "start": 102.081,
   "end": 106.997,
   "text": "Joseph Greene was the Jefferson County Sheriff.\nHis deputies carried the writs."
  },
  {
   "start": 107.047,
   "end": 110.565,
   "text": "Also named in the suit were the Housing Authority\nitself"
  },
  {
   "start": 110.315,
   "end": 115.006,
   "text": "and several public officials responsible\nfor particular Louisville projects."
  },
  {
   "start": 115.056,
   "end": 118.868,
   "text": "The deputy sheriffs are unnamed in the caption\nof the case —"
  },
  {
   "start": 118.618,
   "end": 121.39,
   "text": "certain known and unknown Deputy Sheriffs."
  },
  {
   "start": 121.44,
   "end": 125.745,
   "text": "The brief filed for the sheriff describes the job\nin one clause:"
  },
  {
   "start": 125.495,
   "end": 130.624,
   "text": "the officer of the court who is charged\nwith serving notice in a forcible entry"
  },
  {
   "start": 130.374,
   "end": 136.154,
   "text": "and detainer action, usually a Jefferson County\nDeputy Sheriff. Usually a deputy."
  },
  {
   "start": 136.204,
   "end": 140.68,
   "text": "Two of the men who did the work get their names\nback later, and only in the dissent."
  },
  {
   "start": 140.73,
   "end": 143.302,
   "text": "So on one side of this case: three tenants."
  },
  {
   "start": 143.352,
   "end": 146.956,
   "text": "On the other: a city housing authority, a county\nsheriff,"
  },
  {
   "start": 146.706,
   "end": 151.193,
   "text": "and the Commonwealth's own statute. Their landlord\nwas not a landlord."
  },
  {
   "start": 151.243,
   "end": 155.039,
   "text": "The body that started these proceedings\nwas a government body —"
  },
  {
   "start": 154.789,
   "end": 157.066,
   "text": "Louisville's own housing authority."
  },
  {
   "start": 157.116,
   "end": 160.477,
   "text": "The men who carried the paper were sworn officers."
  },
  {
   "start": 160.527,
   "end": 165.7,
   "text": "Every step of what follows was done by the State,\nto people the State was housing."
  },
  {
   "start": 165.75,
   "end": 170.206,
   "text": "The question the Supreme Court agreed to answer:\nwhether this statute,"
  },
  {
   "start": 169.956,
   "end": 173.17,
   "text": "as applied to tenants in a public housing project,"
  },
  {
   "start": 172.92,
   "end": 175.576,
   "text": "fails to afford those tenants the notice"
  },
  {
   "start": 175.326,
   "end": 179.874,
   "text": "of proceedings initiated against them required\nby the Due Process Clause"
  },
  {
   "start": 179.624,
   "end": 184.386,
   "text": "of the Fourteenth Amendment. As applied.\nNot in the abstract."
  },
  {
   "start": 184.436,
   "end": 188.213,
   "text": "Consider what a summary eviction actually requires\nof the person"
  },
  {
   "start": 187.963,
   "end": 191.927,
   "text": "on the receiving end. A date. A courtroom."
  },
  {
   "start": 191.977,
   "end": 196.736,
   "text": "Whatever a person has to arrange in order\nto be somewhere at a particular hour"
  },
  {
   "start": 196.486,
   "end": 197.8,
   "text": "on a particular day."
  },
  {
   "start": 197.85,
   "end": 203.162,
   "text": "And before any of that, a single piece\nof information: that the thing is happening."
  },
  {
   "start": 203.212,
   "end": 206.202,
   "text": "That information is what the law calls notice."
  },
  {
   "start": 206.252,
   "end": 211.779,
   "text": "Everything else in a court proceeding —\nthe arguments, the evidence, the judgment —"
  },
  {
   "start": 211.529,
   "end": 213.05,
   "text": "is downstream of it."
  },
  {
   "start": 213.1,
   "end": 217.483,
   "text": "A hearing that a person does not know\nabout is not a hearing they failed to attend."
  },
  {
   "start": 217.533,
   "end": 219.733,
   "text": "It is a hearing that happened to them."
  },
  {
   "start": 219.783,
   "end": 225.123,
   "text": "The Supreme Court put it in one line, borrowed\nfrom a 1914 case called Grannis v."
  },
  {
   "start": 224.873,
   "end": 225.648,
   "text": "Ordean:"
  },
  {
   "start": 225.398,
   "end": 230.528,
   "text": "the fundamental requisite of due process of law\nis the opportunity to be heard."
  },
  {
   "start": 230.578,
   "end": 236.186,
   "text": "Then it added the part that carries this case,\nfrom a 1950 decision called Mullane —"
  },
  {
   "start": 235.936,
   "end": 241.105,
   "text": "that the right to be heard has little reality\nor worth unless one is informed"
  },
  {
   "start": 240.855,
   "end": 246.393,
   "text": "that the matter is pending and can choose\nfor himself whether to appear or default,"
  },
  {
   "start": 246.143,
   "end": 251.165,
   "text": "acquiesce or contest. Little reality or worth.\nNot a technicality."
  },
  {
   "start": 251.215,
   "end": 257.803,
   "text": "The whole substance of the thing. The Kentucky\nstatute at issue was section 454.030."
  },
  {
   "start": 257.853,
   "end": 261.325,
   "text": "It is two sentences long and it is worth hearing\nin full,"
  },
  {
   "start": 261.075,
   "end": 265.673,
   "text": "because almost everything in this case turns\non the order of its clauses."
  },
  {
   "start": 265.723,
   "end": 270.316,
   "text": "If the officer directed to serve notice\non the defendant in forcible entry"
  },
  {
   "start": 270.066,
   "end": 274.718,
   "text": "or detainer proceedings cannot find the defendant\non the premises mentioned"
  },
  {
   "start": 274.468,
   "end": 275.421,
   "text": "in the writ,"
  },
  {
   "start": 275.171,
   "end": 279.0,
   "text": "he may explain and leave a copy of the notice\nwith any member"
  },
  {
   "start": 278.75,
   "end": 282.579,
   "text": "of the defendant's family thereon over sixteen\nyears of age,"
  },
  {
   "start": 282.329,
   "end": 287.131,
   "text": "and if no such person is found he may serve\nthe notice by posting a copy thereof"
  },
  {
   "start": 286.881,
   "end": 291.769,
   "text": "in a conspicuous place on the premises. Read it\nslowly and it sounds careful."
  },
  {
   "start": 291.819,
   "end": 295.063,
   "text": "Three steps, descending. Find the person."
  },
  {
   "start": 295.113,
   "end": 298.382,
   "text": "Failing that, find a family member over sixteen."
  },
  {
   "start": 298.432,
   "end": 304.091,
   "text": "Failing that, post it somewhere conspicuous\non the premises. In practice, the door."
  },
  {
   "start": 304.141,
   "end": 306.202,
   "text": "What does the middle step assume?"
  },
  {
   "start": 306.252,
   "end": 311.167,
   "text": "A member of the defendant's family over sixteen\nyears of age. Somebody home."
  },
  {
   "start": 311.217,
   "end": 315.229,
   "text": "Somebody old enough to understand a court paper\nand to pass it on."
  },
  {
   "start": 315.279,
   "end": 320.266,
   "text": "The statute even lets the officer explain it — he\nmay explain and leave a copy."
  },
  {
   "start": 320.316,
   "end": 324.421,
   "text": "That is a conversation on a doorstep,\nwith a person who lives there."
  },
  {
   "start": 324.471,
   "end": 327.74,
   "text": "The statute is written for a household\nwith somebody in it."
  },
  {
   "start": 327.79,
   "end": 331.337,
   "text": "And the third step is what happens when there\nis not."
  },
  {
   "start": 331.387,
   "end": 336.931,
   "text": "There is one more sentence in section 454.030,\nand it is the shortest."
  },
  {
   "start": 336.981,
   "end": 340.482,
   "text": "The notice shall state the time and place\nof meeting of the court."
  },
  {
   "start": 340.532,
   "end": 345.262,
   "text": "That sentence is the reason the paper exists.\nA date and an address."
  },
  {
   "start": 345.312,
   "end": 349.834,
   "text": "It is the only thing in the procedure that tells\na tenant where to go and when."
  },
  {
   "start": 349.884,
   "end": 354.174,
   "text": "The sheriff's side described the ladder exactly\nthe way the statute reads."
  },
  {
   "start": 354.224,
   "end": 360.043,
   "text": "Personal service first. A family member second.\nPosting as a last resort."
  },
  {
   "start": 360.093,
   "end": 363.083,
   "text": "For all three of them it went all the way down."
  },
  {
   "start": 363.133,
   "end": 365.49,
   "text": "In each instance, the opinion says,"
  },
  {
   "start": 365.24,
   "end": 370.067,
   "text": "notice took the form of posting a copy of the writ\nof forcible entry and detainer"
  },
  {
   "start": 369.817,
   "end": 376.011,
   "text": "on the door of the tenant's apartment. Three\ndoors. No conversation on any doorstep."
  },
  {
   "start": 376.061,
   "end": 378.958,
   "text": "The Supreme Court would not accept\nthat description."
  },
  {
   "start": 379.008,
   "end": 382.626,
   "text": "Not because anyone had lied about the words\nof the statute,"
  },
  {
   "start": 382.376,
   "end": 385.854,
   "text": "but because of what the record showed actually\nhappened"
  },
  {
   "start": 385.604,
   "end": 389.846,
   "text": "when a process server walked up to a door\nin a Louisville housing project."
  },
  {
   "start": 391.396,
   "end": 394.014,
   "text": "Start with what posting physically is."
  },
  {
   "start": 394.064,
   "end": 397.659,
   "text": "The Court defined it in a footnote, because it\nhad to:"
  },
  {
   "start": 397.409,
   "end": 402.168,
   "text": "posting refers to the practice of placing the writ\non the property by use"
  },
  {
   "start": 401.918,
   "end": 407.825,
   "text": "of a thumbtack, adhesive tape, or other means.\nA thumbtack. Adhesive tape."
  },
  {
   "start": 407.875,
   "end": 409.286,
   "text": "Or other means."
  },
  {
   "start": 409.336,
   "end": 413.231,
   "text": "That is the mechanism by which a citizen\nof Kentucky was informed"
  },
  {
   "start": 412.981,
   "end": 417.016,
   "text": "that the State had begun a proceeding to remove\nthem from their home."
  },
  {
   "start": 417.066,
   "end": 420.706,
   "text": "Now the sequence, in the words of the brief filed\nfor the sheriff."
  },
  {
   "start": 420.756,
   "end": 423.818,
   "text": "First, the officer goes to the apartment\nin an effort"
  },
  {
   "start": 423.568,
   "end": 425.975,
   "text": "to effectuate personal in-hand service."
  },
  {
   "start": 426.025,
   "end": 430.14,
   "text": "Second, if the named tenant is absent\nor will not appear at the door,"
  },
  {
   "start": 429.89,
   "end": 432.809,
   "text": "personal in-hand service is made on any member"
  },
  {
   "start": 432.559,
   "end": 435.563,
   "text": "of the tenant's family over sixteen years of age."
  },
  {
   "start": 435.613,
   "end": 440.691,
   "text": "Finally, if no one answers the door, a copy\nof the notice is posted on the premises,"
  },
  {
   "start": 440.441,
   "end": 444.151,
   "text": "usually the door. That is the brief's account\nof itself."
  },
  {
   "start": 444.201,
   "end": 447.795,
   "text": "And then the Court's own sentence, the one\nthat undoes it."
  },
  {
   "start": 447.845,
   "end": 451.058,
   "text": "But if no one is at home at the time\nof that visit,"
  },
  {
   "start": 450.808,
   "end": 455.711,
   "text": "as is apparently true in a good percentage\nof cases, posting follows forthwith."
  },
  {
   "start": 455.761,
   "end": 458.68,
   "text": "Forthwith. The same visit."
  },
  {
   "start": 458.73,
   "end": 463.02,
   "text": "The officer knocks, nobody comes, and there\nis nobody at the door to hand it to."
  },
  {
   "start": 463.07,
   "end": 467.221,
   "text": "So step two evaporates, step three happens,\nand the visit is over."
  },
  {
   "start": 467.271,
   "end": 469.239,
   "text": "The Court set this out plainly."
  },
  {
   "start": 469.289,
   "end": 472.964,
   "text": "Neither the statute, nor the practice\nof the process servers,"
  },
  {
   "start": 472.714,
   "end": 476.57,
   "text": "makes provision for even a second attempt\nat personal service —"
  },
  {
   "start": 476.32,
   "end": 480.592,
   "text": "perhaps at some time of day when the tenant\nis more likely to be at home."
  },
  {
   "start": 480.642,
   "end": 484.861,
   "text": "Not a second visit the next morning. Not one\nafter five o'clock."
  },
  {
   "start": 484.911,
   "end": 486.74,
   "text": "Not one at the weekend."
  },
  {
   "start": 486.79,
   "end": 491.312,
   "text": "One attempt, at whatever hour the one visit\nhappened to fall, and the paper went up."
  },
  {
   "start": 491.362,
   "end": 495.744,
   "text": "The Court had already refused that account,\nin its own words:"
  },
  {
   "start": 495.494,
   "end": 500.603,
   "text": "we reject appellants' characterization\nof the procedure contemplated"
  },
  {
   "start": 500.353,
   "end": 505.793,
   "text": "by section 454.030 as one in which posting is used\nas a method of service only"
  },
  {
   "start": 505.543,
   "end": 508.909,
   "text": "as a last resort. The ladder had one rung."
  },
  {
   "start": 508.959,
   "end": 513.853,
   "text": "There is a detail in the phrase a good percentage\nthat is easy to skate past."
  },
  {
   "start": 513.903,
   "end": 516.15,
   "text": "It is not the Court's estimate."
  },
  {
   "start": 516.2,
   "end": 520.023,
   "text": "The Court put it in quotation marks and sent it\nto a footnote,"
  },
  {
   "start": 519.773,
   "end": 524.654,
   "text": "and the footnote points at a deposition — one\nof the men who carried the papers,"
  },
  {
   "start": 524.404,
   "end": 529.613,
   "text": "under oath. The opinion does not say what time\nof day the process servers came."
  },
  {
   "start": 529.663,
   "end": 534.464,
   "text": "It says only that, apparently, in a good\npercentage of cases, nobody was there."
  },
  {
   "start": 534.514,
   "end": 537.783,
   "text": "None of which, on its own, makes posting\nunconstitutional."
  },
  {
   "start": 537.833,
   "end": 541.659,
   "text": "The Court was careful about this, and the care\nis worth keeping."
  },
  {
   "start": 541.709,
   "end": 544.822,
   "text": "Short of providing personal service, it wrote,"
  },
  {
   "start": 544.572,
   "end": 547.883,
   "text": "posting notice on the door of a person's home\nwould,"
  },
  {
   "start": 547.633,
   "end": 549.931,
   "text": "in many or perhaps most instances,"
  },
  {
   "start": 549.681,
   "end": 553.988,
   "text": "constitute not only a constitutionally acceptable\nmeans of service —"
  },
  {
   "start": 553.738,
   "end": 558.636,
   "text": "but indeed a singularly appropriate and effective\nway of ensuring that a person"
  },
  {
   "start": 558.386,
   "end": 562.58,
   "text": "who cannot conveniently be served personally\nis actually apprised"
  },
  {
   "start": 562.33,
   "end": 566.501,
   "text": "of proceedings against him. Singularly appropriate\nand effective."
  },
  {
   "start": 566.551,
   "end": 571.698,
   "text": "Many or perhaps most. The Court is not hostile\nto a piece of paper on a door."
  },
  {
   "start": 571.748,
   "end": 573.25,
   "text": "In the ordinary case,"
  },
  {
   "start": 573.0,
   "end": 577.292,
   "text": "a notice on your own front door is a perfectly\nsensible way to reach you."
  },
  {
   "start": 577.342,
   "end": 582.139,
   "text": "It is where you live. You will be back.\nThat instinct is old."
  },
  {
   "start": 582.189,
   "end": 583.786,
   "text": "You watch your own door."
  },
  {
   "start": 583.836,
   "end": 587.913,
   "text": "So the question narrows to something much smaller\nand much harder"
  },
  {
   "start": 587.663,
   "end": 589.519,
   "text": "than is posting good enough."
  },
  {
   "start": 589.569,
   "end": 593.416,
   "text": "The question is whether, in these buildings,\non these doors,"
  },
  {
   "start": 593.166,
   "end": 597.968,
   "text": "the paper stayed up long enough to be read.\nThat is a question of fact."
  },
  {
   "start": 598.018,
   "end": 599.661,
   "text": "And there was a record."
  },
  {
   "start": 601.211,
   "end": 605.362,
   "text": "The record was made of depositions\nfrom the process servers themselves."
  },
  {
   "start": 605.412,
   "end": 609.331,
   "text": "The men who put the papers on the doors,\nunder oath, describing their own work."
  },
  {
   "start": 609.381,
   "end": 612.742,
   "text": "One deposition in that footnote records\nthe exchange."
  },
  {
   "start": 612.792,
   "end": 615.901,
   "text": "He was asked whether he was aware of there\nbeing any problem"
  },
  {
   "start": 615.651,
   "end": 620.215,
   "text": "with children ripping the writs off. His answer:\noh, we had plenty of trouble."
  },
  {
   "start": 620.265,
   "end": 626.709,
   "text": "The questioner checked it. You had trouble?\nWith kids, yeah. Yeah."
  },
  {
   "start": 626.759,
   "end": 630.028,
   "text": "Asked whether he had ever seen it, he said he had."
  },
  {
   "start": 630.078,
   "end": 632.79,
   "text": "I have seen them take them off of the door,"
  },
  {
   "start": 632.54,
   "end": 638.359,
   "text": "and I would go back and tell them to put it back.\nThey don't know. They didn't know."
  },
  {
   "start": 638.409,
   "end": 643.556,
   "text": "Asked where, he named one place. Well most\nof that was in Village West."
  },
  {
   "start": 643.606,
   "end": 647.757,
   "text": "Another excerpt in the same footnote gives\na smaller answer to the same question."
  },
  {
   "start": 647.807,
   "end": 652.837,
   "text": "Did you ever see kids pulling them off? Yes.\nDid you see many?"
  },
  {
   "start": 652.887,
   "end": 657.988,
   "text": "No, not too many — I did see it in one place\nover there. Where was that?"
  },
  {
   "start": 658.038,
   "end": 661.746,
   "text": "Village West. How many times did you see\nthat happen?"
  },
  {
   "start": 661.796,
   "end": 664.6,
   "text": "Well, probably a couple of times."
  },
  {
   "start": 664.65,
   "end": 668.244,
   "text": "The same development, named twice in the same\nfootnote."
  },
  {
   "start": 668.294,
   "end": 673.56,
   "text": "The dissent gives the man who said probably\na couple of times a name: Carter Bacon."
  },
  {
   "start": 673.61,
   "end": 677.715,
   "text": "A third excerpt describes the arrangement almost\nadministratively."
  },
  {
   "start": 677.765,
   "end": 682.448,
   "text": "The children — we had problems with children. They\nwould take them off."
  },
  {
   "start": 682.498,
   "end": 686.798,
   "text": "They never took them off when we were present,\nbut we, you know, assume —"
  },
  {
   "start": 686.548,
   "end": 690.192,
   "text": "the Housing Authority told us that they would take\nthem off,"
  },
  {
   "start": 689.942,
   "end": 693.729,
   "text": "so we always put them up high. The Housing\nAuthority told us."
  },
  {
   "start": 693.779,
   "end": 697.094,
   "text": "It came out of a deposition, from the men\nthemselves."
  },
  {
   "start": 697.144,
   "end": 699.575,
   "text": "The Housing Authority, one of them said,"
  },
  {
   "start": 699.325,
   "end": 702.967,
   "text": "had told the servers that the children would take\nthe writs off."
  },
  {
   "start": 703.017,
   "end": 706.078,
   "text": "The Court's summary of all this was one sentence:"
  },
  {
   "start": 705.828,
   "end": 708.333,
   "text": "as the process servers were well aware,"
  },
  {
   "start": 708.083,
   "end": 710.288,
   "text": "notices posted on apartment doors"
  },
  {
   "start": 710.038,
   "end": 714.779,
   "text": "in the area where these tenants lived\nwere not infrequently removed by children"
  },
  {
   "start": 714.529,
   "end": 719.45,
   "text": "or other tenants before they\ncould have their intended effect. Well aware."
  },
  {
   "start": 719.8,
   "end": 724.111,
   "text": "And notice what the Court did not write. It\ndid not write a percentage."
  },
  {
   "start": 724.161,
   "end": 728.844,
   "text": "It wrote a significant number of instances,\nand then, in quotation marks,"
  },
  {
   "start": 728.594,
   "end": 732.512,
   "text": "not infrequently. The opinion never prints a rate\nof removal."
  },
  {
   "start": 732.562,
   "end": 735.703,
   "text": "What the record supported was a description,"
  },
  {
   "start": 735.453,
   "end": 740.103,
   "text": "and the Court used the words the record supported\nand no stronger ones."
  },
  {
   "start": 740.153,
   "end": 745.585,
   "text": "The majority did not weigh the servers' testimony\nagainst a study, or a survey,"
  },
  {
   "start": 745.335,
   "end": 748.83,
   "text": "or a count. The opinion names nothing else."
  },
  {
   "start": 748.88,
   "end": 753.588,
   "text": "The testimony was not all one way, and the dissent\nsaid so directly."
  },
  {
   "start": 753.638,
   "end": 756.721,
   "text": "One server told the court about a warning he\nhad been given."
  },
  {
   "start": 756.771,
   "end": 759.621,
   "text": "I had been warned beforehand that, by Mr."
  },
  {
   "start": 759.371,
   "end": 763.445,
   "text": "Bacon, Carter Bacon, that he suspected — he wasn't\ncertain,"
  },
  {
   "start": 763.195,
   "end": 768.274,
   "text": "but he suspected that on some occasions the writs\nhad been torn off the doors"
  },
  {
   "start": 768.024,
   "end": 772.482,
   "text": "by kids. Then the witness stepped back from it.\nThis is what he told me."
  },
  {
   "start": 772.532,
   "end": 777.169,
   "text": "Whether that is true or not, I don't know. And I\ndon't think that he observed that."
  },
  {
   "start": 777.219,
   "end": 778.673,
   "text": "Then his own account:"
  },
  {
   "start": 778.423,
   "end": 780.412,
   "text": "and the six months I was working"
  },
  {
   "start": 780.162,
   "end": 784.574,
   "text": "at it there was no occasion where I saw anyone\ntear the writs off of the door."
  },
  {
   "start": 784.624,
   "end": 787.171,
   "text": "Six months. Not once."
  },
  {
   "start": 787.221,
   "end": 789.357,
   "text": "That man was Gilbert Brutscher,"
  },
  {
   "start": 789.107,
   "end": 794.691,
   "text": "and the dissent set his answer directly\nagainst Bacon's. Same job, opposite answers."
  },
  {
   "start": 794.741,
   "end": 797.917,
   "text": "The dissent does not say they worked the same\nbuildings."
  },
  {
   "start": 797.967,
   "end": 800.167,
   "text": "Another server put it the same way."
  },
  {
   "start": 800.217,
   "end": 805.297,
   "text": "We always try to put the paper up above where, a,\nsay a small child can't reach it."
  },
  {
   "start": 805.347,
   "end": 809.552,
   "text": "Asked whether he had had complaints about small\nchildren ripping them off,"
  },
  {
   "start": 809.302,
   "end": 813.623,
   "text": "he answered that he had never had a complaint\nand had never seen a child try"
  },
  {
   "start": 813.373,
   "end": 814.606,
   "text": "to rip a notice off."
  },
  {
   "start": 814.656,
   "end": 818.881,
   "text": "And the man who said the Housing Authority\nhad warned them said something else"
  },
  {
   "start": 818.631,
   "end": 821.893,
   "text": "in the same deposition. We always put them\nup high."
  },
  {
   "start": 821.943,
   "end": 827.322,
   "text": "So we never had any problems with that.\nThe majority quoted the first half."
  },
  {
   "start": 827.372,
   "end": 829.433,
   "text": "The dissent quoted the second."
  },
  {
   "start": 829.483,
   "end": 834.516,
   "text": "So: a handful of men, describing a practice they\nhad each seen differently."
  },
  {
   "start": 834.566,
   "end": 837.974,
   "text": "The opinion does not say where or when each\nof them worked."
  },
  {
   "start": 838.024,
   "end": 843.357,
   "text": "That is the entire evidentiary base. What\nthe tenants themselves said was narrow."
  },
  {
   "start": 843.407,
   "end": 846.676,
   "text": "They claim never to have seen these posted\nsummonses."
  },
  {
   "start": 846.726,
   "end": 850.301,
   "text": "They state that they did not learn of the eviction\nproceedings"
  },
  {
   "start": 850.051,
   "end": 852.855,
   "text": "until they were served with writs of possession,"
  },
  {
   "start": 852.605,
   "end": 856.245,
   "text": "executed after default judgments had been entered\nagainst them,"
  },
  {
   "start": 855.995,
   "end": 861.275,
   "text": "and after their opportunity for appeal had lapsed.\nClaim. State."
  },
  {
   "start": 861.325,
   "end": 864.222,
   "text": "Those are the Court's verbs, and the Court kept\nthem."
  },
  {
   "start": 864.272,
   "end": 868.445,
   "text": "It wrote that appellees claim to have suffered\nprecisely such a failure"
  },
  {
   "start": 868.195,
   "end": 872.46,
   "text": "of actual notice. Not did suffer. Claim\nto have suffered."
  },
  {
   "start": 872.51,
   "end": 876.357,
   "text": "Nobody ever tried that question. It never got\nthat far."
  },
  {
   "start": 876.407,
   "end": 879.828,
   "text": "The case arrived at the Supreme Court on summary\njudgment,"
  },
  {
   "start": 879.578,
   "end": 884.056,
   "text": "which means the courts below were deciding\nwhether the procedure could stand"
  },
  {
   "start": 883.806,
   "end": 888.499,
   "text": "at all — not whether the three tenants\nwere telling the truth about their own doors."
  },
  {
   "start": 888.549,
   "end": 890.889,
   "text": "What is established is the practice."
  },
  {
   "start": 890.939,
   "end": 893.275,
   "text": "What is alleged, and never tested,"
  },
  {
   "start": 893.025,
   "end": 896.715,
   "text": "is what happened at three particular apartments\nin 1975."
  },
  {
   "start": 898.265,
   "end": 902.648,
   "text": "The three tenants had no way to reopen\nthe evictions in the state courts."
  },
  {
   "start": 902.698,
   "end": 907.313,
   "text": "The Court's phrase for their position is thus\nwithout recourse in the state courts."
  },
  {
   "start": 907.363,
   "end": 911.489,
   "text": "The judgments were final. The appeal window\nhad closed."
  },
  {
   "start": 911.539,
   "end": 913.832,
   "text": "There was no door left to knock on."
  },
  {
   "start": 913.882,
   "end": 916.006,
   "text": "So they went to federal court,"
  },
  {
   "start": 915.756,
   "end": 920.865,
   "text": "filing a class action in the Western District\nof Kentucky under section 1983 —"
  },
  {
   "start": 920.615,
   "end": 924.063,
   "text": "the statute that lets a citizen sue a state\nofficial"
  },
  {
   "start": 923.813,
   "end": 926.346,
   "text": "for violating a constitutional right."
  },
  {
   "start": 926.396,
   "end": 930.099,
   "text": "They asked for declaratory and injunctive relief:\nnot money,"
  },
  {
   "start": 929.849,
   "end": 933.798,
   "text": "but a ruling that the practice was unlawful\nand an order to stop it."
  },
  {
   "start": 933.848,
   "end": 936.012,
   "text": "The notice procedure, they said,"
  },
  {
   "start": 935.762,
   "end": 940.919,
   "text": "did not satisfy the minimum standards\nof constitutionally adequate notice described"
  },
  {
   "start": 940.669,
   "end": 943.386,
   "text": "in Mullane v. Central Hanover Bank and Trust."
  },
  {
   "start": 943.436,
   "end": 947.215,
   "text": "A class action, because the point was never three\napartments."
  },
  {
   "start": 947.265,
   "end": 949.283,
   "text": "That was the theory of the class:"
  },
  {
   "start": 949.033,
   "end": 952.652,
   "text": "if posting was constitutionally inadequate\non these doors,"
  },
  {
   "start": 952.402,
   "end": 955.31,
   "text": "it was inadequate on every door in the project,"
  },
  {
   "start": 955.06,
   "end": 959.657,
   "text": "and on every door in every project served the same\nway. They lost."
  },
  {
   "start": 959.707,
   "end": 962.206,
   "text": "On cross-motions for summary judgment,"
  },
  {
   "start": 961.956,
   "end": 966.113,
   "text": "the District Court granted judgment\nfor the sheriff and his deputies,"
  },
  {
   "start": 965.863,
   "end": 967.573,
   "text": "in an unreported opinion."
  },
  {
   "start": 967.623,
   "end": 972.099,
   "text": "But it did something in that opinion that ends\nup mattering more than the result."
  },
  {
   "start": 972.149,
   "end": 975.308,
   "text": "What it leaned on was old — a case called Weber,"
  },
  {
   "start": 975.058,
   "end": 978.808,
   "text": "decided by the Sixth Circuit some seventy years\nearlier."
  },
  {
   "start": 978.858,
   "end": 983.452,
   "text": "Weber had upheld posting under the statute\nthat came before section 454.030,"
  },
  {
   "start": 983.202,
   "end": 988.063,
   "text": "on the ground that it was reasonable for the State\nto presume that a notice posted"
  },
  {
   "start": 987.813,
   "end": 992.668,
   "text": "on the door of the building in dispute would give\nthe tenant actual notice in time"
  },
  {
   "start": 992.418,
   "end": 998.098,
   "text": "to contest the action. That was 1909.\nThe presumption was doing all the work."
  },
  {
   "start": 998.148,
   "end": 1001.324,
   "text": "Then the District Court conceded that the ground\nhad moved."
  },
  {
   "start": 1001.374,
   "end": 1004.846,
   "text": "It acknowledged that conditions had changed\nsince Weber,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1004.596,
   "end": 1008.875,
   "text": "and that there was undisputed testimony\nin this case that notices posted"
  },
  {
   "start": 1008.625,
   "end": 1012.677,
   "text": "on the apartment doors of tenants are often\nremoved by other tenants."
  },
  {
   "start": 1012.727,
   "end": 1015.925,
   "text": "Undisputed testimony. Often removed."
  },
  {
   "start": 1015.975,
   "end": 1019.235,
   "text": "Removed by other tenants, the District Court wrote\n—"
  },
  {
   "start": 1018.985,
   "end": 1024.513,
   "text": "the depositions in the footnote had said children.\nThe judge wrote all of that down."
  },
  {
   "start": 1024.563,
   "end": 1028.399,
   "text": "Then he ruled that the procedure\nwas constitutionally sufficient —"
  },
  {
   "start": 1028.149,
   "end": 1032.324,
   "text": "on the ground that posting only comes into play\nafter the officer directed"
  },
  {
   "start": 1032.074,
   "end": 1035.401,
   "text": "to serve notice cannot find the defendant\non the premises."
  },
  {
   "start": 1035.451,
   "end": 1038.58,
   "text": "Which is how the sheriff's side had described\nthe statute."
  },
  {
   "start": 1038.63,
   "end": 1042.595,
   "text": "So how can a step that happens on the first visit\nbe a last resort?"
  },
  {
   "start": 1042.645,
   "end": 1045.821,
   "text": "There is no contradiction in the judge's logic."
  },
  {
   "start": 1045.871,
   "end": 1049.033,
   "text": "It is the same assumption the sheriff's side made"
  },
  {
   "start": 1048.783,
   "end": 1051.103,
   "text": "and the same one the statute makes:"
  },
  {
   "start": 1050.853,
   "end": 1054.805,
   "text": "that a step described as a last resort is in fact\na last resort."
  },
  {
   "start": 1054.855,
   "end": 1059.817,
   "text": "Read the words of the law and it is. Read\nthe depositions and it is not."
  },
  {
   "start": 1059.867,
   "end": 1063.275,
   "text": "The Sixth Circuit reversed, and overruled Weber\nto do it."
  },
  {
   "start": 1063.325,
   "end": 1067.225,
   "text": "Its own decision was seventy years old and pointed\nthe other way,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1066.975,
   "end": 1070.866,
   "text": "and the court of appeals said the ground\nunderneath it had shifted."
  },
  {
   "start": 1070.916,
   "end": 1073.105,
   "text": "There may have been a time, it wrote,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1072.855,
   "end": 1076.832,
   "text": "when posting provided a surer means of giving\nnotice than did mailing."
  },
  {
   "start": 1076.882,
   "end": 1078.432,
   "text": "That time has passed."
  },
  {
   "start": 1078.482,
   "end": 1082.259,
   "text": "The uncontradicted testimony by process servers\nthemselves"
  },
  {
   "start": 1082.009,
   "end": 1086.205,
   "text": "that posted summonses are not infrequently removed\nby persons other"
  },
  {
   "start": 1085.955,
   "end": 1091.145,
   "text": "than those served constitutes effective\nconfirmation of the conclusion that notice"
  },
  {
   "start": 1090.895,
   "end": 1096.111,
   "text": "by posting is not reasonably calculated to reach\nthose who could easily be informed"
  },
  {
   "start": 1095.861,
   "end": 1099.93,
   "text": "by other means at hand. What it did with the case\nmatters."
  },
  {
   "start": 1099.98,
   "end": 1103.328,
   "text": "It reversed the grant of summary judgment\nand remanded the case"
  },
  {
   "start": 1103.078,
   "end": 1108.7,
   "text": "for further proceedings. Reversed and remanded.\nNot decided. Sent back."
  },
  {
   "start": 1108.75,
   "end": 1111.601,
   "text": "The court of appeals also did the arithmetic."
  },
  {
   "start": 1111.651,
   "end": 1114.448,
   "text": "Requiring Kentucky to provide notice by mail"
  },
  {
   "start": 1114.198,
   "end": 1118.542,
   "text": "when personal service proves infeasible\nwill not be overly burdensome."
  },
  {
   "start": 1118.592,
   "end": 1123.508,
   "text": "The cost will be minimal. And it pointed\nat a State that had already done it."
  },
  {
   "start": 1123.558,
   "end": 1128.761,
   "text": "The court then noted with approval the provisions\nof the New York counterpart"
  },
  {
   "start": 1128.511,
   "end": 1133.507,
   "text": "of section 454.030, which provides\nthat when notice is served by posting,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1133.257,
   "end": 1138.505,
   "text": "a copy of the petition must be sent by registered\nor certified mail within a day"
  },
  {
   "start": 1138.255,
   "end": 1143.66,
   "text": "of the posting. The remedy was a stamp,\nand another State was already buying them."
  },
  {
   "start": 1143.71,
   "end": 1146.654,
   "text": "The Supreme Court took the appeal in 1981."
  },
  {
   "start": 1146.704,
   "end": 1152.016,
   "text": "It heard argument on the twenty-third of February\n1982, and decided it that May."
  },
  {
   "start": 1152.066,
   "end": 1153.802,
   "text": "Two lawyers argued it."
  },
  {
   "start": 1153.852,
   "end": 1159.063,
   "text": "Two more filed briefs as friends of the court:\nthe National Housing Law Project,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1158.813,
   "end": 1162.926,
   "text": "and the Antioch School of Law, which urged\nthe Court to affirm."
  },
  {
   "start": 1162.976,
   "end": 1164.535,
   "text": "A dispute about a thumbtack"
  },
  {
   "start": 1164.285,
   "end": 1168.2,
   "text": "in Jefferson County had become a question\nabout how a summary eviction begins"
  },
  {
   "start": 1167.95,
   "end": 1170.192,
   "text": "in every State that served notice this way."
  },
  {
   "start": 1170.242,
   "end": 1173.882,
   "text": "Before the holding, one more thing the sheriff's\nside conceded."
  },
  {
   "start": 1173.932,
   "end": 1177.847,
   "text": "If the landlord had been seeking past-due rent\nrather than possession,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1177.597,
   "end": 1182.423,
   "text": "personal service would be required by Kentucky\nlaw. Money got a person served."
  },
  {
   "start": 1182.473,
   "end": 1184.116,
   "text": "The apartment did not."
  },
  {
   "start": 1185.666,
   "end": 1188.607,
   "text": "The opinion was delivered by Justice Brennan,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1188.357,
   "end": 1191.071,
   "text": "and the standard he reached for was not new."
  },
  {
   "start": 1191.121,
   "end": 1196.585,
   "text": "It came from that 1950 case, Mullane, and it\nwas already thirty-two years old:"
  },
  {
   "start": 1196.335,
   "end": 1201.554,
   "text": "an elementary and fundamental requirement of due\nprocess in any proceeding"
  },
  {
   "start": 1201.304,
   "end": 1205.803,
   "text": "which is to be accorded finality is notice\nreasonably calculated,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1205.553,
   "end": 1207.692,
   "text": "under all the circumstances,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1207.442,
   "end": 1211.673,
   "text": "to apprise interested parties of the pendency\nof the action"
  },
  {
   "start": 1211.423,
   "end": 1217.399,
   "text": "and afford them an opportunity to present\ntheir objections. Reasonably calculated."
  },
  {
   "start": 1217.449,
   "end": 1219.649,
   "text": "Under all the circumstances."
  },
  {
   "start": 1219.699,
   "end": 1224.129,
   "text": "The Court then said what was at stake\nin the plainest words it used all day."
  },
  {
   "start": 1224.179,
   "end": 1229.047,
   "text": "In this case, appellees have been deprived\nof a significant interest in property —"
  },
  {
   "start": 1228.797,
   "end": 1233.831,
   "text": "indeed, of the right to continued residence\nin their homes. Then the test itself."
  },
  {
   "start": 1233.881,
   "end": 1237.492,
   "text": "The sufficiency of notice must be tested\nwith reference to its ability"
  },
  {
   "start": 1237.242,
   "end": 1241.097,
   "text": "to inform people of the pendency of proceedings\nthat affect their interests."
  },
  {
   "start": 1241.147,
   "end": 1245.371,
   "text": "Its effect, the Court said, borrowing a line\nfrom a 1925 case,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1245.121,
   "end": 1250.245,
   "text": "must be judged in the light of its practical\napplication to the affairs of men"
  },
  {
   "start": 1249.995,
   "end": 1254.189,
   "text": "as they are ordinarily conducted. Practical\napplication."
  },
  {
   "start": 1254.239,
   "end": 1257.761,
   "text": "Not the words of the statute. What the thing does."
  },
  {
   "start": 1257.811,
   "end": 1261.578,
   "text": "And the Court had just restated the reason posting\nnormally works,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1261.328,
   "end": 1263.309,
   "text": "by quoting Mullane in a footnote."
  },
  {
   "start": 1263.359,
   "end": 1268.185,
   "text": "The ways of an owner with tangible property\nare such that he usually arranges means"
  },
  {
   "start": 1267.935,
   "end": 1272.154,
   "text": "to learn of any direct attack upon his possessory\nor proprietary rights."
  },
  {
   "start": 1272.204,
   "end": 1276.106,
   "text": "Entry upon real estate in the name of law\nmay reasonably be expected"
  },
  {
   "start": 1275.856,
   "end": 1281.066,
   "text": "to come promptly to the owner's attention.\nThat is the 1950 case, in a footnote."
  },
  {
   "start": 1281.116,
   "end": 1284.106,
   "text": "Then the body of the opinion drew the conclusion\nfrom it."
  },
  {
   "start": 1284.156,
   "end": 1289.019,
   "text": "Upon this understanding, a State may in turn\nconclude that in most cases,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1288.769,
   "end": 1293.811,
   "text": "the secure posting of a notice on the property\nof a person is likely to offer"
  },
  {
   "start": 1293.561,
   "end": 1297.272,
   "text": "that property owner sufficient warning\nof the pendency"
  },
  {
   "start": 1297.022,
   "end": 1301.563,
   "text": "of proceedings possibly affecting his interests.\nThen the turn."
  },
  {
   "start": 1301.613,
   "end": 1306.349,
   "text": "But whatever the efficacy of posting in many\ncases, it is clear that,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1306.099,
   "end": 1308.56,
   "text": "in the circumstances of this case,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1308.31,
   "end": 1313.498,
   "text": "merely posting notice on an apartment door\ndoes not satisfy minimum standards"
  },
  {
   "start": 1313.248,
   "end": 1314.448,
   "text": "of due process."
  },
  {
   "start": 1314.498,
   "end": 1317.429,
   "text": "In a significant number of instances,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1317.179,
   "end": 1322.763,
   "text": "reliance on posting pursuant to the provisions\nof section 454.030 results"
  },
  {
   "start": 1322.513,
   "end": 1327.101,
   "text": "in a failure to provide actual notice\nto the tenant concerned."
  },
  {
   "start": 1327.151,
   "end": 1328.859,
   "text": "And under these conditions,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1328.609,
   "end": 1332.985,
   "text": "notice by posting on the apartment door cannot\nbe considered, in Mullane's words,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1332.735,
   "end": 1336.113,
   "text": "a reliable means of acquainting interested parties\nof the fact"
  },
  {
   "start": 1335.863,
   "end": 1338.221,
   "text": "that their rights are before the courts."
  },
  {
   "start": 1338.271,
   "end": 1341.911,
   "text": "The Court closed the assumption that had held\nthe practice up."
  },
  {
   "start": 1341.961,
   "end": 1345.692,
   "text": "Failure to effect personal service on the first\nvisit, it wrote,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1345.442,
   "end": 1350.147,
   "text": "hardly suggests that the tenant has abandoned\nhis interest in the apartment such"
  },
  {
   "start": 1349.897,
   "end": 1353.775,
   "text": "that mere pro forma notice might be held\nconstitutionally adequate."
  },
  {
   "start": 1353.825,
   "end": 1358.394,
   "text": "On the mails, the Court was careful\nand comparative rather than absolute."
  },
  {
   "start": 1358.444,
   "end": 1360.663,
   "text": "The mails, as Mullane had put it,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1360.413,
   "end": 1364.36,
   "text": "provide an efficient and inexpensive means\nof communication."
  },
  {
   "start": 1364.41,
   "end": 1366.798,
   "text": "Notice by mail in the circumstances"
  },
  {
   "start": 1366.548,
   "end": 1371.318,
   "text": "of this case would surely go a long way toward\nproviding the constitutionally"
  },
  {
   "start": 1371.068,
   "end": 1374.783,
   "text": "required assurance — that the State\nhas not allowed its power"
  },
  {
   "start": 1374.533,
   "end": 1378.152,
   "text": "to be invoked against a person\nwho has had no opportunity"
  },
  {
   "start": 1377.902,
   "end": 1382.094,
   "text": "to present a defense despite a continuing interest\nin the resolution"
  },
  {
   "start": 1381.844,
   "end": 1384.883,
   "text": "of the controversy. And then the shape of it."
  },
  {
   "start": 1384.933,
   "end": 1388.821,
   "text": "Particularly where the subject matter\nof the action also happens"
  },
  {
   "start": 1388.571,
   "end": 1391.179,
   "text": "to be the mailing address of the defendant,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1390.929,
   "end": 1393.586,
   "text": "and where personal service is ineffectual,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1393.336,
   "end": 1397.699,
   "text": "notice by mail may reasonably be relied upon\nto provide interested persons"
  },
  {
   "start": 1397.449,
   "end": 1400.044,
   "text": "with actual notice of judicial proceedings."
  },
  {
   "start": 1400.094,
   "end": 1404.384,
   "text": "The apartment they were trying to take\nwas the place they would have got the letter."
  },
  {
   "start": 1404.434,
   "end": 1408.724,
   "text": "Then the Court named the failure exactly, and it\nnamed it in Mullane's words."
  },
  {
   "start": 1408.774,
   "end": 1412.776,
   "text": "The State's continued exclusive reliance\non an ineffective means"
  },
  {
   "start": 1412.526,
   "end": 1415.46,
   "text": "of service is not notice reasonably calculated"
  },
  {
   "start": 1415.21,
   "end": 1419.148,
   "text": "to reach those who could easily be informed\nby other means at hand."
  },
  {
   "start": 1419.198,
   "end": 1421.352,
   "text": "Continued exclusive reliance."
  },
  {
   "start": 1421.402,
   "end": 1422.344,
   "text": "The holding:"
  },
  {
   "start": 1422.094,
   "end": 1426.568,
   "text": "in failing to afford appellees adequate notice\nof the proceedings against them"
  },
  {
   "start": 1426.318,
   "end": 1428.804,
   "text": "before issuing final orders of eviction,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1428.554,
   "end": 1432.986,
   "text": "the State has deprived them of property without\nthe due process of law required"
  },
  {
   "start": 1432.736,
   "end": 1436.905,
   "text": "by the Fourteenth Amendment. So had the Court\nbanned posting?"
  },
  {
   "start": 1436.955,
   "end": 1441.498,
   "text": "It did not, and it said so in terms, underlining\nthe limit:"
  },
  {
   "start": 1441.248,
   "end": 1444.767,
   "text": "in light of the findings of the courts below,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1444.517,
   "end": 1447.728,
   "text": "we hold only that posted notice pursuant"
  },
  {
   "start": 1447.478,
   "end": 1452.32,
   "text": "to section 454.030 is constitutionally inadequate.\nOnly."
  },
  {
   "start": 1452.37,
   "end": 1456.31,
   "text": "In light of the findings below. It did not order\nthe mail either."
  },
  {
   "start": 1456.36,
   "end": 1459.74,
   "text": "It is not our responsibility to prescribe the form\nof service"
  },
  {
   "start": 1459.49,
   "end": 1461.579,
   "text": "that the Commonwealth should adopt."
  },
  {
   "start": 1461.629,
   "end": 1463.537,
   "text": "And it gave up more ground:"
  },
  {
   "start": 1463.287,
   "end": 1467.885,
   "text": "even conceding that process served by mail is far\nfrom the ideal means"
  },
  {
   "start": 1467.635,
   "end": 1473.025,
   "text": "of providing the notice the Due Process Clause\nof the Fourteenth Amendment requires."
  },
  {
   "start": 1473.075,
   "end": 1475.275,
   "text": "Far from the ideal means."
  },
  {
   "start": 1475.325,
   "end": 1477.986,
   "text": "The furthest it would go was comparative —"
  },
  {
   "start": 1477.736,
   "end": 1482.612,
   "text": "that posted service accompanied by mail service\nis constitutionally preferable"
  },
  {
   "start": 1482.362,
   "end": 1486.16,
   "text": "to posted service alone. And the case\nwas not over."
  },
  {
   "start": 1486.21,
   "end": 1490.99,
   "text": "What the Supreme Court affirmed was a judgment\nthat had reversed a summary judgment"
  },
  {
   "start": 1490.74,
   "end": 1493.937,
   "text": "and sent the whole thing back for further\nproceedings."
  },
  {
   "start": 1493.987,
   "end": 1497.488,
   "text": "Affirmed does not mean three tenants walked\nout holding a key."
  },
  {
   "start": 1497.538,
   "end": 1500.535,
   "text": "What happened on remand is not in this opinion,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1500.285,
   "end": 1505.265,
   "text": "and neither is anything else about Linnie Lindsey,\nBarbara Hodgens or Pamela Ray."
  },
  {
   "start": 1505.315,
   "end": 1510.88,
   "text": "It simply stops. Three Justices did not agree,\nand the dissent is not a footnote."
  },
  {
   "start": 1510.93,
   "end": 1513.213,
   "text": "It was written by Justice O'Connor,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1512.963,
   "end": 1516.149,
   "text": "joined by the Chief Justice and by Justice\nRehnquist."
  },
  {
   "start": 1516.199,
   "end": 1519.56,
   "text": "The opinion prints no tally of the votes\non the other side."
  },
  {
   "start": 1519.61,
   "end": 1520.776,
   "text": "It opens: today,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1520.526,
   "end": 1524.875,
   "text": "the Court holds that the Constitution prefers\nthe use of the Postal Service"
  },
  {
   "start": 1524.625,
   "end": 1525.804,
   "text": "to posted notice."
  },
  {
   "start": 1525.854,
   "end": 1529.688,
   "text": "The Court reaches this conclusion despite\nthe total absence of any evidence"
  },
  {
   "start": 1529.438,
   "end": 1534.067,
   "text": "in the record regarding the speed and reliability\nof the mails. Then the charge."
  },
  {
   "start": 1534.117,
   "end": 1538.608,
   "text": "The sole ground for the Court's result\nis the scant and conflicting testimony"
  },
  {
   "start": 1538.358,
   "end": 1540.915,
   "text": "of a handful of process servers in Kentucky."
  },
  {
   "start": 1540.965,
   "end": 1542.564,
   "text": "On this flimsy basis,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1542.314,
   "end": 1547.354,
   "text": "the Court confidently overturns the work\nof the Kentucky Legislature, and,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1547.104,
   "end": 1550.41,
   "text": "by implication, that of at least 10 other States."
  },
  {
   "start": 1550.46,
   "end": 1553.914,
   "text": "The District Court had called the same testimony\nundisputed."
  },
  {
   "start": 1553.964,
   "end": 1556.629,
   "text": "And then the point a lawyer notices first."
  },
  {
   "start": 1556.679,
   "end": 1560.411,
   "text": "The Court, the dissent said, does not cite\na single case,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1560.161,
   "end": 1562.31,
   "text": "other than the decision below,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1562.06,
   "end": 1567.041,
   "text": "supporting its position that notice by posting\nis constitutionally inadequate"
  },
  {
   "start": 1566.791,
   "end": 1570.51,
   "text": "in summary eviction proceedings. Not one case."
  },
  {
   "start": 1570.56,
   "end": 1573.573,
   "text": "The dissent counted the legislatures involved:"
  },
  {
   "start": 1573.323,
   "end": 1578.1,
   "text": "at least 11 States authorizing notice in summary\neviction proceedings solely"
  },
  {
   "start": 1577.85,
   "end": 1581.584,
   "text": "by posting or by leaving the notice\nat the tenant's residence."
  },
  {
   "start": 1581.634,
   "end": 1585.063,
   "text": "It listed them in a footnote. At least eleven."
  },
  {
   "start": 1585.113,
   "end": 1590.642,
   "text": "And it read the statute the way the sheriff's side\nhad: personal service first,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1590.392,
   "end": 1595.023,
   "text": "a family member over sixteen second, posting only\nas a last resort."
  },
  {
   "start": 1595.073,
   "end": 1599.198,
   "text": "Both opinions read the same three clauses. One\nread the words."
  },
  {
   "start": 1599.248,
   "end": 1601.17,
   "text": "The other read the depositions."
  },
  {
   "start": 1601.22,
   "end": 1606.114,
   "text": "There is one more thing the Court declined to do,\nand the declining is deliberate."
  },
  {
   "start": 1606.164,
   "end": 1609.156,
   "text": "Notice law had long turned on a category:"
  },
  {
   "start": 1608.906,
   "end": 1614.541,
   "text": "whether an action was against a person\nor against a thing — in personam or in rem."
  },
  {
   "start": 1614.591,
   "end": 1619.367,
   "text": "Which box did a summary eviction belong in?\nThat had been argued for decades."
  },
  {
   "start": 1619.417,
   "end": 1624.311,
   "text": "The Court refused to decide it, exactly as it\nhad refused in 1950."
  },
  {
   "start": 1624.361,
   "end": 1625.398,
   "text": "As in Mullane,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1625.148,
   "end": 1629.837,
   "text": "we decline to resolve the constitutional question\nbased upon the determination"
  },
  {
   "start": 1629.587,
   "end": 1633.876,
   "text": "whether the particular action is more properly\ncharacterized as one in rem"
  },
  {
   "start": 1633.626,
   "end": 1636.985,
   "text": "or in personam. What the paper\ndid was the question."
  },
  {
   "start": 1637.035,
   "end": 1638.454,
   "text": "Earlier, in a footnote,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1638.204,
   "end": 1642.486,
   "text": "the dissent had already accused the majority\nof doing the very thing it disclaimed."
  },
  {
   "start": 1642.536,
   "end": 1646.793,
   "text": "The Court gives lipservice to the principle\nthat it is not our responsibility"
  },
  {
   "start": 1646.543,
   "end": 1649.982,
   "text": "to prescribe the form of service that Kentucky\nshould adopt,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1649.732,
   "end": 1651.563,
   "text": "but then goes on to do just that."
  },
  {
   "start": 1651.613,
   "end": 1653.946,
   "text": "Its last move was institutional,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1653.696,
   "end": 1657.25,
   "text": "and it is the oldest objection in constitutional\nlaw."
  },
  {
   "start": 1657.3,
   "end": 1658.798,
   "text": "The Court, it said,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1658.548,
   "end": 1662.774,
   "text": "seems to forget that we have long since discarded\nthe concept"
  },
  {
   "start": 1662.524,
   "end": 1666.946,
   "text": "that due process authorizes courts to hold laws\nunconstitutional"
  },
  {
   "start": 1666.696,
   "end": 1670.321,
   "text": "when they believe the legislature has acted\nunwisely."
  },
  {
   "start": 1670.371,
   "end": 1673.454,
   "text": "The majority's opinion does not answer that one."
  },
  {
   "start": 1673.504,
   "end": 1677.934,
   "text": "The dissent offered the counter-image\nthat has outlived everything else in it."
  },
  {
   "start": 1677.984,
   "end": 1679.836,
   "text": "It is no secret, after all,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1679.586,
   "end": 1683.528,
   "text": "that unattended mailboxes are subject to plunder\nby thieves."
  },
  {
   "start": 1683.578,
   "end": 1685.632,
   "text": "And unlike the use of the mails,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1685.382,
   "end": 1689.868,
   "text": "posting notice at least gives assurance\nthat the notice has gotten as far"
  },
  {
   "start": 1689.618,
   "end": 1693.091,
   "text": "as the tenant's door. That is a real argument."
  },
  {
   "start": 1693.141,
   "end": 1697.71,
   "text": "A letter can be stolen, or misdelivered, or thrown\nout with the rest of the post."
  },
  {
   "start": 1697.76,
   "end": 1702.629,
   "text": "The majority never proved the mails were better.\nIt proved this door was worse."
  },
  {
   "start": 1702.679,
   "end": 1706.087,
   "text": "Its answer to the mailbox came in a footnote,\nin one line."
  },
  {
   "start": 1706.137,
   "end": 1709.406,
   "text": "The dissent misconstrues the constitutional\nstandard."
  },
  {
   "start": 1709.456,
   "end": 1712.078,
   "text": "The dissent had a second line of attack,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1711.828,
   "end": 1715.0,
   "text": "and it went to the purpose of the procedure\nitself."
  },
  {
   "start": 1715.05,
   "end": 1718.948,
   "text": "Kentucky's forcible entry and detainer action\nis a summary proceeding"
  },
  {
   "start": 1718.698,
   "end": 1722.226,
   "text": "for quickly determining whether or not a landlord\nhas the right"
  },
  {
   "start": 1721.976,
   "end": 1724.495,
   "text": "to immediate possession of leased premises."
  },
  {
   "start": 1724.545,
   "end": 1728.185,
   "text": "Speed is not a defect in it; speed is the design."
  },
  {
   "start": 1728.235,
   "end": 1731.364,
   "text": "And it quoted the Court's own earlier reasoning\nback at it."
  },
  {
   "start": 1731.414,
   "end": 1735.962,
   "text": "Many expenses of the landlord continue to accrue\nwhether a tenant pays his rent"
  },
  {
   "start": 1735.712,
   "end": 1741.009,
   "text": "or not. Speedy adjudication is desirable\nto prevent subjecting the landlord"
  },
  {
   "start": 1740.759,
   "end": 1745.273,
   "text": "to undeserved economic loss and the tenant\nto unmerited harassment"
  },
  {
   "start": 1745.023,
   "end": 1749.844,
   "text": "and dispossession. The means chosen for making\nservice of process,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1749.594,
   "end": 1752.697,
   "text": "the dissent wrote, must be prompt and certain."
  },
  {
   "start": 1752.747,
   "end": 1755.313,
   "text": "The majority answered that in a footnote,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1755.063,
   "end": 1758.291,
   "text": "and the answer is the tightest sentence\nin the opinion."
  },
  {
   "start": 1758.341,
   "end": 1761.337,
   "text": "From the perspective of the tenant, it wrote,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1761.087,
   "end": 1765.023,
   "text": "it is difficult to see how a means of serving\nprocess that fails"
  },
  {
   "start": 1764.773,
   "end": 1768.135,
   "text": "to afford actual notice in a not insubstantial\nnumber"
  },
  {
   "start": 1767.885,
   "end": 1770.898,
   "text": "of cases can be deemed either prompt or certain."
  },
  {
   "start": 1770.948,
   "end": 1774.077,
   "text": "That is where the two opinions stop talking\nto each other."
  },
  {
   "start": 1775.627,
   "end": 1780.335,
   "text": "What the case leaves is smaller than a rule\nand more durable than a remedy."
  },
  {
   "start": 1780.385,
   "end": 1783.535,
   "text": "Not a rule about envelopes. A method."
  },
  {
   "start": 1783.585,
   "end": 1787.609,
   "text": "In this case the evidence was a handful of men\ndescribing their own work,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1787.359,
   "end": 1790.708,
   "text": "and one of them repeating what the Housing\nAuthority had said."
  },
  {
   "start": 1790.758,
   "end": 1794.763,
   "text": "That is a very small pile of paper to set\nagainst a statute,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1794.513,
   "end": 1797.37,
   "text": "and the three dissenting Justices said so."
  },
  {
   "start": 1797.42,
   "end": 1801.525,
   "text": "It is also, on this record, the only evidence\nanybody brought."
  },
  {
   "start": 1801.575,
   "end": 1804.519,
   "text": "The statute described a ladder with three rungs."
  },
  {
   "start": 1804.569,
   "end": 1808.07,
   "text": "The men who climbed it did not describe it\nthe same way as each other."
  },
  {
   "start": 1808.12,
   "end": 1810.478,
   "text": "Both descriptions were in the record,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1810.228,
   "end": 1814.221,
   "text": "and the Court chose the one made by the men\nwho said the paper came off."
  },
  {
   "start": 1814.271,
   "end": 1820.304,
   "text": "Somewhere in Louisville in 1975 an officer fixed\na writ to a door — by thumbtack,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1820.054,
   "end": 1824.877,
   "text": "or adhesive tape, or other means; the opinion\ndoes not say which."
  },
  {
   "start": 1824.927,
   "end": 1829.332,
   "text": "He did what the statute told him to do. The paper\nwas on the premises."
  },
  {
   "start": 1829.382,
   "end": 1831.118,
   "text": "The service was complete."
  },
  {
   "start": 1831.168,
   "end": 1834.881,
   "text": "Everything after that — the date, the courtroom,\nthe judgment,"
  },
  {
   "start": 1834.631,
   "end": 1838.663,
   "text": "the order of possession — followed correctly\nfrom a single assumption."
  },
  {
   "start": 1838.713,
   "end": 1841.006,
   "text": "That the paper would still be there."
  }
 ],
 "graphics": [],
 "figures": [
