   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 906.689,
   "end": 912.689,
   "kind": "acttitle",
   "title": "NO DOOR LEFT TO KNOCK ON",
   "kicker": "ACT FOUR",
   "index": 4
  },
  {
   "start": 923.036,
   "end": 929.036,
   "kind": "lowerthird",
   "primary": "WESTERN DISTRICT OF KENTUCKY",
   "secondary": "A class action under section 1983 — the statute that lets a citizen sue a state official for violating a constitutional right"
  },
  {
   "start": 939.383,
   "end": 945.383,
   "kind": "kinetic",
   "lines": [
    "NOT MONEY.",
    "A RULING, AND AN ORDER",
    "TO STOP."
   ],
   "style": "emphasis",
   "emphasisWords": [
    "NOT MONEY"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 955.73,
   "end": 961.73,
   "kind": "kinetic",
   "lines": [
    "THEY LOST."
   ],
   "style": "emphasis",
   "emphasisWords": [
    "LOST"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 972.077,
   "end": 978.077,
   "kind": "quote",
   "quote": "it was reasonable for the State to presume that a notice posted on the door of the building in dispute would give the tenant actual notice in time to contest the action",
   "attribution": "Weber (6th Cir. 1909), as described in Greene v. Lindsey"
  },
  {
   "start": 988.424,
   "end": 994.424,
   "kind": "numberticker",
   "value": 1909,
   "label": "The year of Weber, the case the District Court leaned on",
   "group": false
  },
  {
   "start": 1004.771,
   "end": 1010.771,
   "kind": "quote",
   "quote": "there is undisputed testimony in this case that notices posted on the apartment doors of tenants are often removed by other tenants",
   "attribution": "The District Court, quoted in Greene v. Lindsey"
  },
  {
   "start": 1021.118,
   "end": 1027.118,
   "kind": "kinetic",
   "lines": [
    "UNDISPUTED.",
    "OFTEN REMOVED.",
    "AND STILL SUFFICIENT."
   ],
   "style": "emphasis",
   "emphasisWords": [
    "AND STILL SUFFICIENT"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 1037.466,
   "end": 1043.466,
   "kind": "quote",
   "quote": "that posting only comes into play after the officer directed to serve notice cannot find the defendant on the premises",
   "attribution": "The District Court's ground, quoted in Greene v. Lindsey"
  },
  {
   "start": 1053.813,
   "end": 1059.813,
   "kind": "compbars",
   "items": [
    {
     "label": "The statute, read as words",
     "value": 3
    },
    {
     "label": "The statute, read through the depositions",
     "value": 1
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 1070.16,
   "end": 1076.16,
   "kind": "lowerthird",
   "primary": "THE SIXTH CIRCUIT",
   "secondary": "Reversed the summary judgment, and overruled its own 1909 decision to do it"
  },
  {
   "start": 1086.507,
   "end": 1092.507,
   "kind": "quote",
   "quote": "a time when posting provided a surer means of giving notice than did mailing. That time has passed.",
   "attribution": "The Sixth Circuit, quoted in Greene v. Lindsey — overruling Weber"
  },
  {
   "start": 1102.854,
   "end": 1108.854,
   "kind": "lowerthird",
   "primary": "REVERSED AND REMANDED",
   "secondary": "Not decided. Sent back for further proceedings."
  },
  {
   "start": 1119.201,
   "end": 1125.201,
   "kind": "quote",
   "quote": "Requiring Kentucky to provide notice by mail when personal service proves infeasible will not be overly burdensome. The cost will be minimal.",
   "attribution": "The Sixth Circuit, quoted in Greene v. Lindsey, n. 2"
  },
  {
   "start": 1135.548,
   "end": 1141.548,
   "kind": "lowerthird",
   "primary": "NEW YORK'S COUNTERPART",
   "secondary": "When notice is served by posting, a copy of the petition must be sent by registered or certified mail within a day"
  },
  {
   "start": 1151.895,
   "end": 1157.895,
   "kind": "casetimeline_c",
   "events": [
    {
     "year": "1981",
     "text": "The Supreme Court notes probable jurisdiction"
    },
    {
     "year": "23 Feb 1982",
     "text": "Argued"
    },
    {
     "year": "17 May 1982",
     "text": "Decided — No. 81-341"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 1168.242,
   "end": 1174.242,
   "kind": "kinetic",
   "lines": [
    "MONEY GOT A PERSON SERVED.",
    "THE APARTMENT DID NOT."
   ],
   "style": "emphasis",
   "emphasisWords": [
    "THE APARTMENT DID NOT"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 1202.988,
   "end": 1208.988,
   "kind": "acttitle",
   "title": "DEFEND IT, THEN OVERTURN IT",
   "kicker": "ACT FIVE",
   "index": 5
  },
  {
   "start": 1237.133,
   "end": 1243.133,
   "kind": "quote",
   "quote": "appellees have been deprived of a significant interest in property: indeed, of the right to continued residence in their homes",
   "attribution": "Greene v. Lindsey (1982)"
  },
  {
   "start": 1271.278,
   "end": 1277.278,
   "kind": "quote",
   "quote": "the secure posting of a notice on the property of a person is likely to offer that property owner sufficient warning of the pendency of proceedings possibly affecting his interests",
   "attribution": "Greene v. Lindsey (1982), on why posting is normally enough"
  },
  {
   "start": 1305.423,
   "end": 1311.423,
   "kind": "quote",
   "quote": "But whatever the efficacy of posting in many cases, it is clear that, in the circumstances of this case, merely posting notice on an apartment door does not satisfy minimum standards of due process.",
   "attribution": "Greene v. Lindsey, 456 U.S. 444 (1982) — the turn"
  },
  {
   "start": 1339.567,
   "end": 1345.567,
   "kind": "kinetic",
   "lines": [
    "IN A SIGNIFICANT",
    "NUMBER OF INSTANCES,",
    "IT FAILED."
   ],
   "style": "emphasis",
   "emphasisWords": [
    "IT FAILED"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 1373.712,
   "end": 1379.712,
   "kind": "quote",
   "quote": "Notice by mail in the circumstances of this case would surely go a long way toward providing the constitutionally required assurance",
   "attribution": "Greene v. Lindsey (1982), on the mails"
  },
  {
   "start": 1407.857,
   "end": 1413.857,
   "kind": "quote",
   "quote": "the State's continued exclusive reliance on an ineffective means of service is not notice reasonably calculated to reach those who could easily be informed by other means at hand",
   "attribution": "Greene v. Lindsey (1982), closing on Mullane's words"
  },
  {
   "start": 1442.002,
   "end": 1448.002,
   "kind": "quote",
   "quote": "In light of the findings of the courts below, we hold only that posted notice pursuant to section 454.030 is constitutionally inadequate.",
   "attribution": "Greene v. Lindsey (1982), n. 9 — stating its own limit"
  },
  {
   "start": 1476.146,
   "end": 1482.146,
   "kind": "kinetic",
   "lines": [
    "IT DID NOT BAN POSTING.",
    "IT DID NOT ORDER THE MAIL."
   ],
   "style": "emphasis",
   "emphasisWords": [
    "DID NOT"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 1510.291,
   "end": 1516.291,
   "kind": "lowerthird",
   "primary": "JUSTICE O'CONNOR, DISSENTING",
   "secondary": "Joined by the Chief Justice and by Justice Rehnquist"
  },
  {
   "start": 1544.436,
   "end": 1550.436,
   "kind": "quote",
   "quote": "The sole ground for the Court's result is the scant and conflicting testimony of a handful of process servers in Kentucky. On this flimsy basis, the Court confidently overturns the work of the Kentucky Legislature and, by implication, that of at least 10 other States.",
   "attribution": "The dissent"
  },
  {
   "start": 1578.581,
   "end": 1584.581,
   "kind": "numberticker",
   "value": 11,
   "label": "States, at least, authorizing notice in summary eviction proceedings solely by posting or by leaving it at the tenant's residence — the dissent's count",
   "group": false
  },
  {
   "start": 1612.726,
   "end": 1618.726,
   "kind": "quote",
   "quote": "As in Mullane, we decline to resolve the constitutional question based upon the determination whether the particular action is more properly characterized as one in rem or in personam.",
   "attribution": "Greene v. Lindsey (1982) — the majority declining the category question"
  },
  {
   "start": 1646.87,
   "end": 1652.87,
   "kind": "quote",
   "quote": "we have long since discarded the concept that due process authorizes courts to hold laws unconstitutional when they believe the legislature has acted unwisely",
   "attribution": "The dissent, quoting Ferguson v. Skrupa (1963)"
  },
  {
   "start": 1681.015,
   "end": 1687.015,
   "kind": "quote",
   "quote": "It is no secret, after all, that unattended mailboxes are subject to plunder by thieves. Moreover, unlike the use of the mails, posting notice at least gives assurance that the notice has gotten as far as the tenant's door.",
   "attribution": "The dissent"
  },
  {
   "start": 1715.16,
   "end": 1721.16,
   "kind": "kinetic",
   "lines": [
    "SPEED IS NOT A DEFECT IN IT.",
    "SPEED IS THE DESIGN."
   ],
   "style": "emphasis",
   "emphasisWords": [
    "SPEED IS THE DESIGN"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 1749.305,
   "end": 1755.305,
   "kind": "quote",
   "quote": "from the perspective of the tenant, it is difficult to see how a means of serving process that fails to afford actual notice in a not insubstantial number of cases can be deemed either prompt or certain",
   "attribution": "Greene v. Lindsey (1982), n. 4 — the majority answering the dissent"
  },
  {
   "start": 1779.354,
   "end": 1785.354,
   "kind": "kinetic",
   "lines": [
